"""quartz: a shopping agent for one shopper in an ORO SN15 store world; it starts from the public YesYesYes v1
agent (which builds on Know Thyself v1 and KYO v6). It works only through the store's own tools, whatever
their names, argument shapes and result formats, and it is set up afresh from the request and the tool list
in every episode.

How an episode runs:
- The brief: the light model reads the request and the tool descriptions into items (name, store label, quantity,
  firm requirements), the budget, the pick rule, message and step limits, the steps the request asks for along the
  way, and the role of each tool (search, open, offer, compare, message, cart, cart view, checkout, decline). The
  heavy model reads it again when that first reading is incomplete or doubtful.
- The loop: the light model calls the store's tools under one guide prompt. Code keeps a ledger of every listing
  record seen, the requirements in force and the cart as tracked from the calls, and appends it to each turn with
  the steps left. It skips calls that would repeat a view, reads further pages of a search for an extreme pick,
  joins single opens into one side-by-side view, and keeps the last steps for finishing.
- The interview: once enough listings are read, a planner ranks the properties worth asking about for each item
  from the request and the attribute keys seen; questions go to the shopper one property per message, each answer
  is read into requirements, and searches with the new values follow.
- Before a checkout: offers are read again, sold-out lines are swapped, requirements whose value sits on another
  listing are followed page by page, accessory fit and the cart lines are checked against the request, cheaper rows
  are looked for when the cheapest is wanted, and a checking model reviews the cart; any problem holds the order
  with a concrete reason.
- Before ending without a purchase: a review of the records against the requirements, recovery searches for items
  still without a fit, and holds that name the listings that may fit; a confirmed lead is carted and bought by code.
- The finish: when the loop stops with the episode open, code checks out a held cart, else ends without a
  purchase with neutral wording, after the steps the request asks for at that point.

quartz's own changes:
- Fixed order: the loops over sets whose order reaches a prompt or a call are sorted, so runs repeat.
- The checkout and no-purchase tools never carry questions to the shopper (keep_closers()).
- Listing records written as text ("key: value | key: value", JSON text, "name=value; ..." attributes) and
  attributes given as a list of name/value entries are read as records (_unflatten(), _line_record(), _listed_facts()).
- The decline audit sees each item's best opened candidates, not only the newest (audit_pick()).
- A decline audit that comes back empty (the light model spent its whole budget reasoning) is asked once more at low
  reasoning effort, so a decline is not taken unchecked (audit_call()).
- Before the first order each cart line is given a backup chosen from listings already read (same store category,
  buyable, inside the budget with the rest of the cart, holding as many of the item's required values); after a
  checkout refused for a sold-out line or a new budget, Kyo swaps the line for its backup with the cart tool and
  checks out again (keep_backups(), backup_for(), repair_cart()). Their prices come from cost_of(), which also reads
  a record's one price key when the base has not learned the store's price key. An unchanged cart is never sent again after a
  stock notice while a swap can be made.
- A cart line from a neighbouring category is held once when the request names the item's store label word for word
  and a listing with that exact label can be bought inside the budget (label_faults()); labels are compared by their
  last path part, and a line whose own label the request names is never held.
- Compatibility: a part series after any short phrase, exceptions to a series, and fit lists continued on another
  part's page (no veto until that page is read).
- A page call the store answers with a fault (a small map whose key names an error, fault, failure or trouble) does
  not count as an opened page (failed()).

Why these changes, and why so few: the base won race 16 by doing well in three different worlds in a row, so it is
kept as it is wherever no change applies. Each change answers something seen in several races: checkouts refused
by a sold-out line, a price or a new budget (8 to 36 tasks a race), faults written as bare codes or status texts, a
neighbouring category whose label holds the requested one's words, compatibility written as a part series, and
orders that depended on the order of a set. A change stays silent when its trigger is absent, so the same
episode sends the same calls as the base, which free replays against the base's own runs check.
"""
